# (category, difficulty, question, correct, [wrong x3], explanation)
AG='agentic-architecture-and-orchestration'; TL='tool-design-and-mcp-integration'
CC='claude-code-configuration-and-workflows'; PE='prompt-engineering-and-structured-output'
CX='context-management-and-reliability'
Q = [
# ---- Orchestration safeguards (0%) ----
(AG,'hard',"A support agent loop is capped at 15 iterations. In production 3% of sessions hit the cap and the customer sees a generic timeout with no ticket created. Which change most directly meets the requirement that every session ends resolved or escalated?",
 "Wrap the loop in orchestration code so the cap, timeout, max_tokens and exception paths all call one escalation handler that files a ticket with a handoff package",
 ["Raise the cap to 40 iterations so fewer sessions reach it","Add a system-prompt line telling the model to escalate before it runs out of iterations","Retry the whole session silently from the first turn when the cap is reached"],
 "Termination guarantees belong in code that owns every exit path. Raising the cap only moves the failure, a prompt instruction is probabilistic, and silent restarts repeat side effects and hide the failure."),
(AG,'hard',"An agent session ends with stop_reason max_tokens while the model was mid-way through a tool_use block for issue_refund. What must the orchestrator do?",
 "Treat the turn as incomplete: never execute the partial call, retry with a larger limit, and escalate if it still cannot complete",
 ["Execute the call with whatever arguments were parsed so far","Present the truncated text to the customer as the resolution","Treat max_tokens like end_turn and close the session as resolved"],
 "A truncated tool_use block has unreliable arguments, and a financial action must never run on it. max_tokens is a distinct exit path that needs its own handling."),
(AG,'medium',"Which set of loop-exit conditions must an orchestration layer handle explicitly so no session is left without an outcome?",
 "end_turn, iteration cap, max_tokens, tool failure or timeout, refusal and unhandled exceptions, each mapped to resolve or escalate",
 ["end_turn only, because the model decides when it is done","end_turn and tool_use only, since the others are rare","Only the iteration cap, because it bounds every other case"],
 "The loop can terminate for many reasons other than end_turn. A guarantee needs a mapped outcome for each one, including exceptions outside the model."),
(AG,'hard',"A tool call hangs for 90 seconds, then the agent process is killed by the platform. The customer's refund was approved but never issued and no one was notified. Which safeguard would have prevented the silent failure?",
 "Per-tool timeouts plus a session-level deadline and a persisted session state, so a watchdog escalates any session that has no terminal outcome",
 ["A longer system-prompt reminder to verify refunds were issued","Increasing the model's thinking budget so it detects hangs sooner","Logging the session ID so engineers can review failures next week"],
 "Hangs and crashes happen outside the model's reasoning. Deadlines plus durable state let a separate watchdog guarantee an outcome; logging alone is passive."),
(AG,'hard',"A design review proposes: 'the agent will decide when a case is too hard and call escalate_to_human.' What is the principal weakness?",
 "Escalation depends on the model choosing to call the tool, so loop caps, failures and timeouts that bypass that choice can still end with no resolution or handoff",
 ["The escalate tool will be called too often and overload the human queue","Tools cannot be used to trigger human handoff in an agent loop","Models cannot recognise hard cases under any circumstances"],
 "An escalation tool is useful, but it is one path. The guarantee requires orchestration code to escalate on every non-resolved termination, whatever the model does."),
# ---- Iterative refinement (0%) ----
(CC,'medium',"Claude Code keeps formatting changelog entries inconsistently despite a long prose description of the desired style. What is the most effective next step?",
 "Provide two or three concrete input and expected-output examples, including an edge case",
 ["Rewrite the prose description with more adjectives and emphasis","Repeat the instruction in capital letters at the start and end of the prompt","Ask Claude to try again until the output looks right"],
 "Concrete input/output examples communicate the transformation precisely. More prose and emphasis stay ambiguous and unmeasurable."),
(CC,'hard',"A developer is refining a parsing function. Four reported failures interact: fixing one date format changes how timezone offsets are handled. How should the feedback be delivered?",
 "Describe all four interacting failures in one message with failing examples so they are fixed and evaluated together",
 ["Send one failure per message so each fix is isolated","Ask for a full rewrite and review only the final result","Fix them in separate sessions to avoid context pollution"],
 "Interacting issues must be considered together or fixes undo each other. Independent issues can go sequentially; interacting ones are batched."),
(CC,'medium',"For a new data-validation function with a clear specification, which workflow gives the most reliable iterative refinement?",
 "Write the tests first, run them, then give Claude the specific failing cases to fix",
 ["Generate the code and manually inspect it for correctness","Ask for the implementation and tests in one step without running either","Write a longer specification and skip tests until release"],
 "Tests turn feedback into targeted, objective failures. Without them, refinement is guesswork."),
# ---- Enforce vs CLAUDE.md (0%) ----
(CC,'hard',"CLAUDE.md says 'never edit files under /migrations'. An audit shows Claude edited a migration in 2 of 50 sessions. What is the correct fix?",
 "Add a permissions deny rule for edits under /migrations, or a PreToolUse hook that blocks them, and keep CLAUDE.md for explanation",
 ["Move the sentence to the top of CLAUDE.md and capitalise it","Add the instruction to a Skill so it loads on demand","Add the rule to the system prompt of every subagent"],
 "CLAUDE.md is context the model can weigh, so compliance is not guaranteed. A must-never rule belongs in deterministic enforcement."),
(CC,'medium',"Which of these belongs in CLAUDE.md rather than in settings permissions or a hook?",
 "Prefer functional components and describe the project's test naming convention",
 ["Never read the .env file","Block any Bash command that runs rm -rf","Run the formatter after every file edit"],
 "Conventions and preferences are advisory guidance. The other three are must-always or must-never rules that need deterministic enforcement."),
(CC,'hard',"A security team requires that no developer can run git push --force from Claude Code, even if they edit their own project settings. Which mechanism fits?",
 "A deny rule in managed settings, which takes precedence over user and project settings",
 ["A line in each repository's CLAUDE.md forbidding force pushes","A deny rule in each developer's ~/.claude/settings.json","A Skill that explains why force pushes are dangerous"],
 "Managed settings are set by the organisation and cannot be overridden by lower scopes. User-level files and CLAUDE.md can be edited or ignored."),
(CC,'hard',"A team wants gofmt and unit tests to run after every Claude file edit without relying on the model remembering. Which design is correct?",
 "A PostToolUse hook matching Edit and Write tools that runs the formatter and tests",
 ["A CLAUDE.md section titled 'Always run gofmt and tests'","A slash command developers invoke when they finish","A PreToolUse hook that runs before the edit happens"],
 "PostToolUse fires after the tool and is independent of model compliance. A slash command depends on people, and PreToolUse runs before the file changes."),
# ---- Project context methods (0%) ----
(CC,'medium',"A rule set about API error conventions applies to every task for the whole team across sessions. Where should it live?",
 "In the project CLAUDE.md, imported from a separate file with an @ import if it is long",
 ["Typed inline at the start of each session","An @ reference in each individual prompt","A personal note in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md"],
 "Reusable, cross-session, team-wide context belongs in project CLAUDE.md. Inline or per-prompt references must be repeated, and a user file is not shared."),
(CC,'medium',"You want Claude to compare two specific existing files for this one task only. Which method fits best?",
 "@ references to the two files in the prompt",
 ["Add both files to CLAUDE.md so they load every session","Paraphrase both files from memory inline","Create a hook that injects both files on every prompt"],
 "An @ reference pulls named files into this prompt without permanently loading them."),
(CC,'hard',"A 900-line CLAUDE.md is loaded in every session and is degrading focus. Which restructuring is best?",
 "Split it into focused files imported with @ and move file-type conventions into .claude/rules with path globs so they load only when relevant",
 ["Delete all guidance older than a month","Move everything into one long system prompt","Convert every section into a slash command"],
 "Modular imports and path-scoped rules keep always-on context small and load specifics only when needed."),
# ---- Escalation criteria (0%) ----
(CX,'medium',"A customer writes 'Just get me a human.' The agent has a tool that could probably solve the issue. What should it do?",
 "Escalate immediately with a handoff summary, without first attempting to resolve it",
 ["Try the tool first and escalate only if it fails","Ask the customer to explain the problem again before deciding","Offer a discount and continue the conversation"],
 "An explicit request for a human is honoured immediately. Investigating first overrides the customer's stated choice."),
(CX,'hard',"A customer says 'this is ridiculous, I'm so annoyed' but has not asked for a person, and the issue is within policy. What is the best behaviour?",
 "Acknowledge the frustration, offer to resolve the issue, and escalate if they repeat the request or the agent cannot progress",
 ["Escalate immediately because negative sentiment was detected","Ignore the emotion and continue silently with the task","End the session politely and open a ticket for later"],
 "Frustration alone is not a trigger. Acknowledging it while attempting resolution is right, with escalation if they ask or progress stalls."),
(CX,'hard',"A customer asks for a price match against a competitor, but policy says nothing about competitor pricing. What should the agent do?",
 "Escalate, because the policy has a gap and the agent should not invent a rule",
 ["Approve the match because it keeps the customer happy","Deny the request and cite the closest policy","Offer a smaller discount the agent estimates is fair"],
 "A policy gap is a genuine escalation trigger. Improvising either way creates inconsistent, unauthorised outcomes."),
(CX,'medium',"A customer-lookup tool returns three accounts for 'Maria Silva'. What should the agent do?",
 "Ask the customer for an additional identifier such as an email or order number",
 ["Pick the account with the most recent activity","Pick the first result and proceed","Escalate to a human immediately"],
 "Heuristic selection risks acting on the wrong customer. Asking for a distinguishing identifier resolves the ambiguity safely."),
(CX,'hard',"A team proposes escalating whenever the model's self-reported confidence is below 0.7 or sentiment analysis turns negative. What is the best assessment?",
 "Both are unreliable proxies, so use explicit triggers: customer request, policy gap, repeated failed attempts or no progress",
 ["It is sound because confidence is well calibrated","It is sound if the threshold is tuned monthly","It is only unsound for sentiment, not for confidence"],
 "Self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated and sentiment does not map to resolvability. Concrete observable triggers are more dependable."),
# ---- tool_choice, schema enforcement, sequencing (0%) ----
(PE,'medium',"A pipeline parses every response as JSON. With tool_choice auto, 4% of responses contain a conversational sentence instead of a tool call. Which fix guarantees a tool call?",
 "Set tool_choice to {\"type\":\"tool\",\"name\":\"extract_invoice\"} so that tool is always called",
 ["Keep tool_choice auto and add 'always call the tool' to the prompt","Set tool_choice to none and parse the text","Lower the temperature to 0"],
 "Forcing a named tool guarantees invocation. A prompt instruction or temperature setting cannot guarantee it."),
(PE,'hard',"What is the difference between tool_choice any and a forced named tool?",
 "any guarantees some tool is called and the model chooses which; a named tool guarantees that specific tool",
 ["any lets the model reply in text; a named tool does not","They are identical, only the syntax differs","any forbids tool calls and named forces exactly two"],
 "any removes the text-only option but leaves the choice of tool to the model. A named tool fixes both."),
(PE,'hard',"A developer enables extended thinking and sets tool_choice to a forced named tool. What should they expect?",
 "An API error, since forced tool use is not compatible with extended thinking; only auto or none is allowed",
 ["Both features work together with no change","The tool is called but thinking is silently disabled","Thinking tokens are billed twice"],
 "Extended thinking supports only auto and none for tool_choice. Choose structured output without thinking, or use auto with a schema-bearing tool and validate."),
(TL,'hard',"A workflow must call get_customer before issue_refund. In testing the model sometimes calls issue_refund with a guessed customer ID. Which design is most reliable?",
 "Enforce the order in code: reject issue_refund until a verified get_customer result exists, optionally forcing get_customer first with tool_choice",
 ["Number the tools in the prompt and say 'follow the order'","Rename issue_refund to issue_refund_after_get_customer","Raise the temperature so the model explores the correct order"],
 "Prerequisite order on a consequential action must be enforced programmatically. Prompt order and naming are probabilistic."),
(PE,'hard',"Tool use with a JSON schema returns valid JSON, yet totals occasionally do not equal the sum of line items. What does this show?",
 "A schema enforces syntax, not semantic correctness, so add validation such as a sum check and retry with the specific error",
 ["The schema is broken and must be regenerated","tool_choice was not forced","The model must be switched to prefill mode"],
 "Schema conformance guarantees shape. Semantic checks need explicit validation and targeted retries."),
# ---- Nullable / optional schemas ----
(PE,'hard',"An extraction schema marks purchase_order_number as required. On invoices without one, the model returns plausible-looking invented numbers. What is the best fix?",
 "Make the field nullable or optional and instruct the model to return null when the document does not contain it",
 ["Add 'do not make up values' to the prompt and keep the field required","Add an enum of ten common PO prefixes","Increase max_tokens so the model has room to check"],
 "Required fields force a value. Nullable fields give an honest way to represent missing data."),
(PE,'medium',"A document_type enum has four values, but some documents fit none and others are unreadable. Which schema design handles this best?",
 "Add 'other' with a free-text detail field and 'unclear' to the enum",
 ["Keep the four values so the schema stays strict","Convert the enum to an unconstrained string","Make document_type required and default to the first value"],
 "'other' plus detail and 'unclear' prevent forced misclassification while keeping a structured, queryable field."),
(PE,'hard',"A date field returns '03/04/2024' in some records and 'April 3, 2024' in others. Which combination best improves consistency?",
 "Add format-normalisation instructions plus few-shot examples of varied source formats mapped to ISO 8601",
 ["Add a longer description of what dates are","Switch to free-form output and parse with regex","Lower max_tokens"],
 "Normalisation rules with examples across formats anchor the model's behaviour; vague description does not."),
# ---- Exploration, stateless, descriptions, resume, plan, decomposition, MCP ----
(CC,'medium',"You are new to a 2,000-file repository and need every caller of processPayment. Which tool sequence is best?",
 "Grep for processPayment to find callers, then Read only the relevant files, following imports as needed",
 ["Read every file in src/ first","Glob for all .js files and read them in order","Ask the model to recall the call graph from training"],
 "Grep finds content, Read opens specific files, and incremental reading keeps context usage low."),
(CC,'medium',"What is the difference between Grep and Glob in Claude Code?",
 "Grep searches file contents; Glob matches file paths by pattern",
 ["Grep matches paths; Glob searches contents","They are aliases for the same operation","Grep edits files; Glob reads them"],
 "Use Glob to find files by name pattern and Grep to find text inside files."),
(CX,'medium',"A chat application sends only the latest user message to the Messages API and the model forgets earlier context. Why?",
 "The Messages API is stateless, so the application must resend the relevant conversation history on every request",
 ["The model has a bug that resets context after each turn","The API stores history only for 5 minutes","History is kept automatically if the same API key is used"],
 "The API keeps no conversation state. The caller owns and resends history each time."),
(CX,'hard',"Where should canonical conversation history be stored in a multi-user web application?",
 "Server-side, keyed by session, with the server constructing each request so clients cannot forge assistant or tool_result turns",
 ["In the browser, with the client sending the whole history each time","In the system prompt, rewritten by the model","In an environment variable"],
 "Client-held history can be tampered with. The server should own the canonical history and role structure."),
(TL,'hard',"Two tools, search_orders and lookup_order_status, are frequently confused. Where should the first fix be applied?",
 "Expand each tool's description with purpose, input formats, examples and an explicit when-to-use-this-versus-that note",
 ["Add a router model before the agent","Merge them into a single tool with 14 parameters","Increase the number of few-shot conversations in the system prompt"],
 "Descriptions are the primary selection mechanism, so fix overlap there before adding infrastructure."),
(TL,'medium',"A teammate cannot see an MCP server you configured and it needs the same server for the shared code review process. Which scope is correct?",
 "Project scope in a committed .mcp.json, using ${ENV_VAR} for credentials",
 ["User scope in ~/.claude.json","Pasting the server URL and token into CLAUDE.md","A local-scope entry for your working directory"],
 "Shared tooling lives in the project's .mcp.json. Secrets come from environment variable expansion, not committed values."),
(CC,'hard',"After a --resume of a long analysis session, three source files were modified by a teammate. What is the best way to restore accuracy without redoing everything?",
 "Resume the session and tell the agent exactly which files changed so it re-analyses only those",
 ["Resume and assume prior findings still hold","Discard the session and restart from scratch","Re-read every file in the repository"],
 "Targeted re-analysis keeps earlier valid work and corrects only the stale parts. If tool results are broadly stale, start fresh with an injected summary."),
(CC,'medium',"When is plan mode the better choice than direct execution?",
 "For large, multi-file or architecturally uncertain changes, or when stakeholders must review the approach before implementation",
 ["For a one-line typo fix with a known location","For renaming a variable inside one function","Whenever the developer wants faster output"],
 "Plan mode suits uncertainty, breadth and review needs. Small, clear, reversible changes are fine to execute directly."),
(AG,'medium',"An agent is asked to improve test coverage in an unfamiliar codebase. Which approach to decomposition is best?",
 "Map the structure, prioritise high-impact areas, and generate and revise subtasks as findings arrive",
 ["Fix a predefined list of ten steps regardless of what is found","Process files alphabetically","Ask one subagent to do everything in a single prompt"],
 "Open-ended work needs adaptive decomposition. Fixed chains only suit predictable tasks."),
(AG,'hard',"A resumed agent re-runs three expensive tool calls whose results it already had. Which design avoids this?",
 "Persist tool results or a structured findings summary and inject it on resume, re-running only the steps whose inputs changed",
 ["Rely on the model's memory of the earlier session","Re-run everything to be safe","Delete the session history and start again"],
 "Durable findings plus context injection restores state without repeating prior work."),
(TL,'hard',"An MCP tool returns 'Error: failed' for every failure. The agent keeps retrying a rule violation. What should the tool return instead?",
 "A structured error with category, a retryable flag, a message and a suggested next action, with isError set",
 ["A longer plain-text apology","An empty result so the agent stops","An HTTP 500 for every case"],
 "Structured, typed errors let the agent decide to retry, reroute or escalate. Business-rule violations should be non-retryable."),
(CX,'hard',"A handoff to a human agent consists of 'customer is upset about order'. What is missing?",
 "Customer ID, root cause, actions already taken, amounts, authorisation state and a recommended next action",
 ["A transcript of the full conversation is the only requirement","The model's chain of thought","A satisfaction score"],
 "The human has no transcript. A structured package preserves the facts and authority state needed to act."),
]
